    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d219">

                <pb facs="0220" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg210"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./257: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-16T12:00:00+09:00">July 16,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received July 16—4:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1006. The following is a paraphrase of a secret telegram received today from
                    London by my British colleague.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>It is learnt that the Japanese Ambassador at Vichy has been instructed to
                        present to the French Government a demand for bases in French Indochina. The
                        demand is to be made with a time limit.<anchor n="89" xml:id="d219-f1"/> In
                        case the French Government accepts the demand the Japanese occupation will
                        be carried out peacefully and territorial integrity guarantees will be
                        given. Matériel, goods and arms will be promised. In case of French refusal
                        the occupation will be carried out by force and Japanese Ambassador has been
                        directed to hint that important changes for Indochina may be
                        involved.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Japanese attach great importance to the United States and Great
                        Britain having no prior information for fear complications might be created.
                        The French Ambassador therefore is not being informed lest he inform his
                        American and British colleagues.<anchor n="90" xml:id="d219-f2"/>
                    </item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d219-f1" n="89" xml:id="d219-f1-content">
                <p>This was later identified as July 20.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f2" n="90" xml:id="d219-f2-content">
                <p>The Adviser on Political Relations (Hornbeck) wrote in a memorandum dated July
                    16: “This has an important bearing on the question whether to act immediately by
                    way of prevention or to defer action and then take it in what would be in a
                    retaliatory or punitive sense.” In a footnote notation Dr. Hornbeck added that
                    he and the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs (Hamilton) “do <hi
                        rendition="#italic">not</hi> recommend” immediate preventative action. For
                    Ambassador Grew’s review of the situation, see telegram No. 1015, July 17, 11
                    a.m., <ref target="frus1941v04#pg_1006">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >iv</hi>, p. 1006</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
